From cc36ec54740f1c50c9f7f2135cb0b87b5f509af1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 19:01:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 30/32] libmount: refuse X-mount.subdir for non-root users

X-mount.subdir= is implemented by mounting the filesystem root on a
temporary target in an unshared namespace, binding the subdirectory to
the final mount point and unmounting the root again.  That is a sequence
of mount(2) and move_mount() calls with the target given by pathname,
which is exactly what the target pinning avoids everywhere else -- the
path is resolved by the kernel over and over and any component of it can
be swapped by the unprivileged user in between.

Upstream solves this by keeping the option working on Linux >= 6.15,
where the subdirectory can be opened directly on the detached tree and
the whole hookset collapses into a single open_tree() call.  That code
does not exist in this branch, so the option is simply refused for
restricted users here.

Root is not affected and keeps the current implementation.

This is a behaviour change: a "user" fstab entry with X-mount.subdir=
stops working for unprivileged users and mount(8) reports "filesystem
was mounted, but any subsequent operation failed: Operation not
supported".  Nothing is left mounted, the wording comes from the generic
hook failure path in mnt_context_get_mount_excode().

Backport notes:

 * Taken from the upstream stable/v2.42 branch, which records the master
   commit in its own message.

 * The Linux >= 6.15 fast path the upstream commit is placed after does
   not exist here (it came with ce4e181aa, which is not applicable to
   this branch -- see the CVE-2026-78409 analysis), so the guard is the
   first thing hook_mount_pre() does and the option is refused on every
   kernel, not only before 6.15.  The debug message and the manual page
   wording are adjusted accordingly.

 * No separate tracker; the option was never covered by a CVE, this is
   the mitigation for the unsafe multi-step implementation.

Signed-off-by: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
(cherry picked from commit 8bbd56b396ebb8449e5a39391c784df35919827b)
Signed-off-by: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
---
 libmount/src/hook_subdir.c | 10 ++++++++++
 sys-utils/mount.8.adoc     |  2 ++
 2 files changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libmount/src/hook_subdir.c b/libmount/src/hook_subdir.c
index 2a2b8c2a4..e2a2ddf71 100644
--- a/libmount/src/hook_subdir.c
+++ b/libmount/src/hook_subdir.c
@@ -290,6 +290,16 @@ static int hook_mount_pre(
 	if (!hsd)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* The unshare + string-based move_mount() dance below is not safe for
+	 * restricted users; the safe variant (open the subdirectory directly
+	 * on the detached tree) needs Linux >= 6.15 and is not available in
+	 * this branch. */
+	if (mnt_context_target_fd_required(cxt)) {
+		DBG(HOOK, ul_debugobj(hs,
+			"subdir mount refused for non-root user"));
+		return -ENOTSUP;
+	}
+
 	/* create unhared temporary target */
 	hsd->org_target = strdup(mnt_fs_get_target(cxt->fs));
 	if (!hsd->org_target)
diff --git a/sys-utils/mount.8.adoc b/sys-utils/mount.8.adoc
index 8ef21d5c6..059731cef 100644
--- a/sys-utils/mount.8.adoc
+++ b/sys-utils/mount.8.adoc
@@ -727,6 +727,8 @@ Allow mounting sub-directory from a filesystem instead of the root directory. Fo
 +
 Note that this feature will not work in session with an unshared private mount namespace (after *unshare --mount*) on old kernels or with *mount*(8) without support for file-descriptors-based mount kernel API. In this case, you need *unshare --mount --propagation shared*.
 +
+This option is not supported for unprivileged (non-root) users. The temporary root-directory mount and the following bind are done by several *mount*(2) syscalls with the target given by pathname, which cannot be protected against a path component swapped in the meantime.
++
 This feature is EXPERIMENTAL.
 
 *X-mount.owner*=_username_|_UID_, *X-mount.group*=_group_|_GID_::
